  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $0\\le 2$ and $4.3$ is not irrational\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\u00ac(A\u2227B)=\u00acA\u2228\u00acB\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(0\\le 2\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}4.3\\text{\u00a0is not irrational}) & \u2261\u00ac((0\\le 2)\u2227(4.3\\text{\u00a0is not irrational})) \\\\  & \u2261\u00ac(0\\le 2)\u2228\u00ac(4.3\\text{\u00a0is not irrational}) \\\\  & \u2261(0>2)\u2228(4.3\\text{\u00a0is irrational}) \\\\  & \u2261(0>2)\\text{\u00a0or\u00a0}4.3\\text{\u00a0is irrational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
